.TH REMEDIA 1 "2026-10-08" "Remedia 1.3.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
remedia \- modular Linux environment for media production,
system diagnostics, and recovery

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B remedia
.RI < command > " [args]"

.B remedia

.B remedia system center

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Remedia
is a modular system management and media production
environment designed primarily for Debian and Ubuntu Linux.

Remedia integrates command-line tools, runtime services,
system diagnostics, backup and recovery utilities,
and video production workflows.

The project combines a unified CLI, modular execution
environment, module registry, and system configuration
into a single engineering framework.

Remedia operates on top of the host Linux operating
system and does not replace the Linux kernel,
systemd, or system package managers.

.SH COMMANDS

.TP
.B setup
Configure Remedia and initialize its working environment.

.TP
.B doctor
Run system diagnostics and report the health
of supported system components.

.TP
.B backupkit
Access backup and recovery utilities,
including user data and system recovery tools.

.TP
.B demo
Run system simulations and workflow tests.

.TP
.B mediapanel
Launch the video production and media management interface.

.TP
.B mediasystem
Configure the media production environment,
including GPU optimization and multimedia tools.

.TP
.B system
Access system monitoring, diagnostics,
maintenance, and repair functions.

.SH QUICK START

Display the main command help:

.EX
remedia
.EE

Launch Remedia System Center:

.EX
remedia system center
.EE

Run system diagnostics:

.EX
remedia doctor
.EE

Open the media production panel:

.EX
remedia mediapanel
.EE

Access backup and recovery utilities:

.EX
remedia backupkit
.EE

.SH ARCHITECTURE

Remedia uses a modular architecture consisting of:

.TP
.B CLI
Main command router and entry point.

.TP
.B Runtime
Execution environment for Remedia components.

.TP
.B Registry
Module registration and integration contracts.

.TP
.B Modules
Independent functional components,
including system tools, MediaSystem,
MediaPanel, BackupKit, and Demo.

.TP
.B Configuration
System configuration stored under /etc/remedia.

.SH SYSTEM COMPONENTS

.SS MediaSystem
Provides tools for preparing and maintaining
a Linux multimedia production environment.

.SS MediaPanel
Provides a video production workflow
covering media ingestion, processing,
editing preparation, and export.

.SS BackupKit
Provides backup and recovery tools
for user data and supported system components.

.SS System Tools
Provides diagnostic, monitoring,
maintenance, and repair utilities.

.SH STORAGE LAYOUT

Remedia supports separating working data,
source media, and backup archives
across multiple storage devices.

Recommended locations:

.TP
.B /mnt/shotcut
Fast working storage for proxy files
and temporary project data.

.TP
.B /mnt/storage
Storage for original media files
and production projects.

.TP
.B /mnt/backups
Storage for backup archives and recovery data.

The storage layout should be prepared
before installing Remedia.

.SH FILES

.TP
.B /etc/remedia/remedia.env
Main system configuration file.

.TP
.B /usr/bin/remedia
Main command-line entry point.

.TP
.B /usr/lib/remedia
Remedia runtime, core components,
and installed modules.

.SH DIAGNOSTICS

Remedia provides diagnostic tools
for monitoring supported system components.

Run the global doctor:

.EX
remedia doctor
.EE

Diagnostic results may include component
status information and overall health indicators.

System repair operations may require
additional privileges.

.SH DEVELOPMENT MODEL

Remedia is evolving towards:

.IP \(bu 2
Manifest-driven system state management.

.IP \(bu
Transactional operations with state tracking.

.IP \(bu
Rollback and recovery mechanisms.

.IP \(bu
Improved runtime isolation.

.IP \(bu
Reproducible system environments.

These capabilities represent development directions
and should not be interpreted as fully implemented
features.

.SH SAFETY

Remedia can interact with system components,
filesystems, package management tools,
and user data.

Users should maintain current backups
and understand the effects of system-level operations.

Testing in a safe environment is recommended
before applying potentially disruptive changes.

